                %li
                  The problem with ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo −
                        %mi Mod
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R-Mod
                  ’ is that it contains a minus sign, not a dash at all. I've been removing minus signs from ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo −
                        %mi Mod
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R-Mod
                  ’, ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mi Vect
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k-Vect
                  ’, ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-Cat
                  ’ etc because they seem entirely wrong, and replacing them with nothing on the grounds that nothing is needed there (except a space, which should come automatically) in a mathematical formula. If you really want a minus sign, let me know, and I won't change it. If you want a dash, you can use
                  %code $R$-$Mod$
                  (which won't work inside a displayed equation),
                  = succeed "," do
                    %code $R\text{-}Mod$
                  %code $R&amp;#x2010;Mod$
                  (which is a little funky but arguably the most proper). I will change minus signs to one of those from now on, if that is what you prefer. (Of course,
                  %code $_R Mod$
                  also works.) Compare: ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo −
                        %mi Mod
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R-Mod
                  ’, ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mi Mod
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Mod
                  ’, ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
